  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CERF and Sudden Onset</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">As a flexible and fully un-earmarked humanitarian pooled fund it is also well aligned with Ireland&#x2019;s Grand Bargain commitment to un-earmarked funding. In order to address any sudden onset crisis or emergency that may arise, a humanitarian funding reserve is maintained to ensure that Ireland is in a position to respond rapidly and flexibly, either through a call for proposals or bilateral engagement with a pre-identified partner, depending on which organisations are best placed to respond.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Central Emergency Response Fund (CERF) is a UN humanitarian funding instrument, managed by the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (UNOCHA). It releases money rapidly when a crisis occurs or when there is a sudden escalation of an existing crisis and responds to underfunded emergencies. It is a rapid response mechanism that aims to have funds on the ground within 72 hours of a crisis occurring, thereby kick-starting life-saving humanitarian response. It also strengthens core elements of humanitarian response in underfunded crises by responding to critical gaps.</narrative>
  </description>
